Aim
To understand Python strings and lists and develop a program to parse machine log data, classify parts and generate shift reports.

Objectives
Extract part code, diameter and shift from a log line (IT12).

Store 10 part diameters and calculate count, average, minimum and maximum (IT10).

Classify parts into ACCEPT, REWORK and SCRAP categories (IT8).

Compare day and night shift reports using sets (IT6).

Concepts Used
Strings and string methods: strip(), split(), upper()

Type conversion using float()

Lists and list operations

For loops and conditional statements

Sets and set operations

Minimum, maximum and average calculations

Parsing Process
Read the raw machine log.

Remove unwanted spaces using strip().

Separate fields using split(";").

Extract part code, diameter and shift.

Convert diameter into a floating-point number.

Store and classify the extracted data.

Quality Classification
Diameter (mm)

Classification

24.95–25.05

ACCEPT

24.90–24.94 or 25.06–25.10

REWORK

Below 24.90 or above 25.10

SCRAP

Container Justification
List: Stores multiple diameters and preserves order.

Set: Identifies common and unique scrap part codes.

Tuple: Suitable for fixed data that should not change.

Inspection and Errors
Extra spaces: handled using strip().

Incorrect diameter: may cause ValueError.

Missing field: may cause IndexError.

Blank line: requires validation before parsing.

Result
Successfully studied string parsing, list storage, data filtering and set operations for generating production shift reports.

Conclusion
Python strings and containers help convert unstructured machine log data into meaningful production information and quality-control reports.
